.TH AWKI 1 "2026" "AWK Interpreter" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
awki \- pattern scanning and text processing language (Rust, Go, and Crystal implementations)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B awk_rust
[\fB\-F\fR \fIvalue\fR] [\fB\-v\fR \fIvar=value\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR] \fI'program'\fR [\fIfile ...\fR]
.PP
.B awk_go
[\fB\-F\fR \fIvalue\fR] [\fB\-v\fR \fIvar=value\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR] \fI'program'\fR [\fIfile ...\fR]
.PP
.B awk_crystal
[\fB\-F\fR \fIvalue\fR] [\fB\-v\fR \fIvar=value\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR] \fI'program'\fR [\fIfile ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
AWK is a language for data manipulation, text retrieval, and prototyping algorithms.
This interpreter is built from scratch in three languages \(em Rust, Go, and Crystal.
All three produce identical output for the same input.
.PP
An AWK program is a sequence of \fIpattern { action }\fR pairs and function definitions.
Short programs are entered on the command line in single quotes.
Longer programs can be read from a file with \fB\-f\fR.
Input is read from files given on the command line, or from standard input when none are given.
Input is broken into records by \fBRS\fR (default: newline).
Each record is compared against each pattern; on a match, the action is executed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-F " value"
Set the field separator \fBFS\fR to \fIvalue\fR.
.TP
.BI \-v " var=value"
Assign \fIvalue\fR to program variable \fIvar\fR. Multiple \fB\-v\fR options are allowed.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read the program from \fIfile\fR instead of the command line.
.SH "THE AWK LANGUAGE"
.SS Program Structure
A program is a sequence of \fIpattern { action }\fR rules.
If pattern is omitted, the action runs on every record.
If action is omitted, the matching record is printed.
.nf

    { print }              # every line
    /Sam/ { print }        # lines matching regex
    NR > 1 { print }       # expression pattern
.fi
.SS Special Patterns
.TP
.B BEGIN
Runs once before any input is read.
.TP
.B END
Runs once after all input is processed.
.TP
.B BEGINFILE / ENDFILE
Runs once at the start and end of each input file.
.TP
.B pat1, pat2
Range pattern \(em matches all records from pat1 to pat2, inclusive.
.SS Fields and Records
.TP
.B $0
Entire current record.
.TP
.B $1, $2, ..., $n
nth field (split by FS).
.TP
.B $NF
Last field.
.SS Built-in Variables
.TS
l l l.
Variable	Default	Meaning
FS	" "	Input field separator
OFS	" "	Output field separator
RS	"\\n"	Input record separator
ORS	"\\n"	Output record separator
NF	\-	Number of fields in current record
NR	\-	Current record number (all files)
FNR	\-	Record number within current file
FILENAME	\-	Name of current input file
RSTART	\-	Start position set by match()
RLENGTH	\-	Length set by match()
.TE
.SS Operators
Arithmetic: \fB+ \- * / % ^\fR
.br
Comparison: \fB== != < <= > >=\fR
.br
Logical: \fB&& || !\fR
.br
Regex: \fB~\fR (matches)  \fB!~\fR (does not match)
.br
Assignment: \fB= += \-= *= /= %= ++ \-\-\fR
.br
Ternary: \fIcond\fR \fB?\fR \fItrue\fR \fB:\fR \fIfalse\fR
.br
Concatenation: place values next to each other.
.SS Statements
.TP
.B print
Prints fields separated by OFS, followed by ORS.
.nf
    { print }
    { print $1, $2 }
    { print "Name:", $1 }
.fi
.TP
.B printf
Formatted output. Does not add a newline automatically.
.nf
    { printf "%s scored %d\\n", $1, $2 }
.fi
Specifiers: \fB%s\fR string, \fB%d\fR integer, \fB%f\fR float, \fB%e\fR scientific, \fB%g\fR shorter of %f/%e, \fB%%\fR literal %.
.TP
.B Output Redirection
.nf
    { print > "file" }     # overwrite
    { print >> "file" }    # append
    { print | "cmd" }      # pipe
.fi
.SS Control Flow
.TP
.B if / else
.nf
    if (expr) { ... } else if (expr) { ... } else { ... }
.fi
.TP
.B while
.nf
    while (expr) { ... }
.fi
.TP
.B for
.nf
    for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) { ... }
.fi
.TP
.B for-in
.nf
    for (key in array) { ... }
.fi
.TP
.B next
Skip remaining rules and move to the next record.
.TP
.B exit
Stop processing immediately. END block still runs.
.TP
.B break / continue
Used inside loops.
.SS Arrays
AWK arrays are associative (key \(-> value). Keys can be strings or numbers.
.nf
    { count[$1]++ }
    END { for (k in count) print k, count[k] }
    delete arr[key]
    if (key in arr) { print "found" }
.fi
.SS Built-in String Functions
.TP
.BI length( s )
Length of s; length of $0 if no argument.
.TP
.BI substr( s , start [ , len ])
Substring of s from position start (1-based), optionally of length len.
.TP
.BI index( s , t )
Position of t in s; 0 if not found.
.TP
.BI split( s , arr , sep )
Split s into array arr by sep; returns count.
.TP
.BI sub( regex , repl , s )
Replace first match of regex in s with repl.
.TP
.BI gsub( regex , repl , s )
Replace all matches of regex in s with repl.
.TP
.BI match( s , regex )
Position of regex in s; sets RSTART and RLENGTH.
.TP
.BI tolower( s )
Convert s to lowercase.
.TP
.BI toupper( s )
Convert s to uppercase.
.TP
.BI sprintf( fmt , ... )
Format string like printf; return as string.
.SS Built-in Math Functions
\fBsin\fR(x), \fBcos\fR(x), \fBatan2\fR(y,x), \fBexp\fR(x), \fBlog\fR(x), \fBsqrt\fR(x), \fBint\fR(x), \fBrand\fR(), \fBsrand\fR(x)
.SS User-Defined Functions
.nf
    function name(params,    locals) {
        ...
        return value
    }
.fi
Extra parameters after a gap of spaces act as local variables.
Functions can call other functions and themselves recursively.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Print every line
awk_rust '{print}' file.txt

# Print first field
awk_rust '{print $1}' file.txt

# Filter by field value
awk_rust '$2 > 70 {print $1}' file.txt

# Sum a column
awk_rust '{sum += $2} END {print "Total:", sum}' file.txt

# Average a column
awk_rust '{sum += $2} END {print "Average:", sum/NR}' file.txt

# CSV input
awk_rust -F, '{print $1, $3}' file.csv

# Pre-set variable
awk_rust -v threshold=70 '$2 > threshold {print $1}' file.txt

# Program from file
awk_rust -f program.awk file.txt

# Count occurrences
awk_rust '{count[$1]++} END {for (k in count) print k, count[k]}' file.txt

# Grade report with user-defined function
awk_rust '
function grade(s) {
    if (s >= 75) return "distinction"
    if (s >= 50) return "pass"
    return "fail"
}
{ print $1, grade($2) }' file.txt
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR awk (1),
.BR gawk (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Built from scratch in Rust, Go, and Crystal as part of a language implementation project.
